162:**Ring seat (one seat for US 7 to 13).** The ring (the real model, read-only) is rolled 30 degrees about its band axis, so the four pads face straight down onto the four pins. The band, a circle of radius R0 = bore radius + 2.6 mm, rests on two flat flanks, each inclined 20 degrees, with their contact lines 3.7 to 4.7 mm on each side of the pad row (strip 3.3 to 5.8 mm; band touches at +-4.2 mm at US 9). The bore centre of every size lies on the vertical line through the pad row, so the pad stays at the same place; it moves by 0.157 mm in height from US 7 to US 13 (flank geometry, CALC `(R0 - R9) x (1/cos 20 - 1)`), which the pin height tolerates. The flanks are modelled 0.01 mm below the band so that the model has no touching faces; the measured vertical gap at the contact is 0.008 mm for every size.
3:Version F3, 2026-11-10. Follows `design/architecture.md` version A5-draft (read 2026-11-10: section 17 is the current ring; where sections disagree, 17.8 wins over 17.7, then 17.1 to 17.6, then 16, then the rest), `design/case/case-architecture.md` version C1, `design/ecad/ring/sch-notes.md` and `requirements.md`. The hardware is fixed; where it blocks a requirement, the conflict is in "Hardware issues found" at the end. The owner's rule that never changes: "The ring shouldn't CONSTANTLY record, only when a long press on it. Then you have a live storing audio".
